    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-what">
      <h2>What Is an Apple Body Shape?</h2>
      <p>An apple body shape has a bust and hips that are roughly the same width, with a waist that stays close to those measurements rather than tapering inward. The fullness that would normally sit at the hips or bust in other shapes shows up around the midsection instead — belly, ribs and sometimes the upper back. Haven't measured yet? Our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> will confirm which of the seven shapes actually fits your numbers.</p>
      <p>The formula: bust and hips need to be within 3.6 inches of each other, and your waist needs to sit within 2 inches of — or larger than — either the bust or the hips. That second part is what separates apple from rectangle. Both shapes have balanced bust and hips; the difference is entirely in how much waist definition is left.</p>
      <div class="abs-note">
        <p><strong>Quick check:</strong> bust and hips within 3.6 inches of each other, waist within 2 inches of (or bigger than) both of them. If your waist is clearly smaller than either one — by more than 2 inches, just not by a dramatic amount — you're more likely a rectangle.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="abs-fig">
        <img src="/assets/images/apple-body-shape-3d-front-side-back.webp" width="1500" height="1136" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Apple body shape 3D illustration from the front, side and back, with bust, waist and hips measurement lines labelled at 38, 38 and 40 inches">
        <figcaption class="abs-figcap">An apple body shape rendered in 3D from the measurements above: waist stays close to bust and hips rather than tapering in. Open the <a href="/body-visualizer/">body visualizer</a> to rotate it yourself and try your own numbers.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Want to see the shape interactively? Open the <a href="/body-visualizer/">body visualizer</a>, choose the female model and tap the <strong>Apple</strong> chip under <em>Try a shape</em> to rotate an apple silhouette from the front, side and back.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-rare">
      <h2>How Common Is the Apple Shape?</h2>
      <p>The most widely cited figures, from a 2006 NC State analysis of over 6,000 women, put the apple shape at roughly 14% — less common than rectangle (~46%) and pear (~20%), but noticeably more common than the hourglass family (~8% combined). Those specific numbers come from a press release rather than a peer-reviewed table, and later re-analyses have found different splits, so treat them as commonly cited rather than exact. Apple is also one of the shapes most misunderstood in casual conversation, often conflated with body size rather than proportion.</p>
      <p><strong>Apple shape is not a weight category.</strong> It describes where your frame carries fullness relative to your own bust and hip measurements — a slim person can be apple-shaped, and a larger person can be a rectangle or hourglass. Two people at very different sizes can both be genuine apples if their waist-to-bust-to-hip relationship matches the formula.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-compare">
      <h2>Apple vs Rectangle — What's the Real Difference?</h2>
      <p>These two get mixed up constantly, because both have a balanced bust and hip measurement. The waist is the entire distinction.</p>
      <div class="abs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Trait</th><th scope="col">Apple</th><th scope="col">Rectangle</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bust vs. hips</td><td>Within 3.6 in of each other</td><td>Within 3.6 in of each other</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waist</td><td>Within 2 in of, or larger than, bust/hips</td><td>More than 2 in smaller, but under 9–10 in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Visual read</td><td>Fullness sits through the middle</td><td>Straight, uniform column top to bottom</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Styling focus</td><td>Elongate the torso, imply a waistline</td><td>Add curves and definition the frame doesn't naturally have</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <figure class="abs-fig">
        <img src="/assets/images/apple-vs-rectangle-body-shape-3d.webp" width="1120" height="1148" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of apple and rectangle body shapes at the same hip size, with bust, waist and hips labelled">
        <figcaption class="abs-figcap">Same balanced bust and hips on both — the only real difference is how much the waist tapers in between them.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>If style advice written for "straight" or "athletic" body types has never quite worked for you, this is often why — rectangle tips assume there's a waist to belt or cinch, and an apple shape doesn't have the same room to work with in that specific spot.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-whr">
      <h2>What Does an Apple Shape Mean for Waist-to-Hip and Waist-to-Height Ratio?</h2>
      <p>Because the waist sits close to (or larger than) the bust and hips, apple shapes tend to run on the higher side of waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) — often somewhere around 0.85 and up for women, at or above the WHO's threshold for elevated cardiovascular and metabolic risk. This is the shape most directly linked to that particular health conversation, because abdominal fat behaves differently, metabolically, from the fat carried in the hips and thighs of a pear shape.</p>
      <p>Waist-to-height ratio (WHtR) is worth checking alongside WHR here specifically, since it doesn't depend on your hip measurement at all — just waist and height. A ratio under 0.5 is the widely cited healthy target. Run both numbers on our <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist to hip ratio calculator</a> and <a href="/waist-to-height-ratio-calculator/">waist to height ratio calculator</a> rather than assuming from shape category alone. The <a href="/body-proportions-calculator/">body proportions calculator</a> shows both ratios together with your bust-to-hip ratio.</p>
      <div class="abs-note">
        <p>None of this is a diagnosis. WHR and WHtR are population-level risk indicators, not individual verdicts — genetics, activity level, diet quality, sleep and stress all matter more than any single ratio. If a number concerns you, the right next step is a conversation with a doctor, not a plan built from a calculator result.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-style">
      <h2>How to Dress for an Apple Body Shape</h2>
      <p>The core rule for this shape is about direction, not concealment: clothing that creates a long, unbroken vertical line — and skims rather than clings through the midsection — works with you, not against you. The sections below work through each category piece by piece, shown on a 3D apple-shaped figure (38–38–40 in) in two versions — one that breaks the vertical line, one that builds it.</p>

      <h3 id="apple-necklines">Necklines</h3>
      <p>A neckline that opens up vertically does more for this shape than almost anything else in the wardrobe, because it lengthens the distance the eye travels before it reaches your midsection. A <strong>V-neckline</strong>, the deeper the better within reason, is the most reliable choice — it draws a clean line downward and makes the whole torso read as longer. A scoop neck does a softer version of the same thing. High, crew, or boat necklines are worth limiting: they sit close to the collarbone and can make the distance between chin and bust look shorter, which puts more visual weight on the midsection rather than less.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-necklines-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of necklines on an apple body shape — avoid: high crew neckline (closes off the chest and shortens the torso); choose: v-neckline (lengthens the neck and draws the eye downward)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="apple-sleeves">Sleeves</h3>
      <p>Sleeves matter here mainly because of where they add width — the goal is to keep the shoulder and upper-arm line fairly streamlined so nothing competes with the vertical lines you’re building elsewhere. A <strong>fitted or tailored sleeve</strong> that follows the arm does this without effort. A three-quarter length is a particularly good option, since it shows a slim part of the arm and adds a natural break in the eye line. Puff, dolman, and heavily gathered sleeves are the ones to be selective with — they add volume right at the widest part of the upper body, which pulls attention back up to an area you’re usually trying to soften.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-sleeves-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of sleeves on an apple body shape — avoid: puff or balloon sleeve (adds bulk level with the fullest part of the torso); choose: fitted, tailored sleeve (streamlined arm keeps the silhouette narrow)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="apple-shirts-t-shirts-and-tops">Shirts, T-Shirts &amp; Tops</h3>
      <p>The most useful word for tops on this shape is <strong>skim</strong>, not fit and not hide — fabric that follows the body loosely enough to move past the midsection without clinging to it. A <strong>wrap top</strong> does this cleanly: the tie creates definition at a point you choose, while the rest of the fabric drapes rather than pulls. A relaxed tunic in a fabric with some drape works the same way over trousers or leggings. Tight, fitted-through-the-body tops are the trickiest choice here, since clingy fabric follows every contour of the midsection rather than skimming past it.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-tops-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of tops on an apple body shape — avoid: tight, clingy top (outlines the midsection exactly); choose: draped v-neck top (falls past the middle without holding onto it)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="apple-knitwear">Knitwear</h3>
      <p>Knitwear can be one of the more forgiving categories here, as long as the fabric has enough drape to move past the body rather than hug it. A <strong>longline, lightweight sweater</strong> that falls past the hip elongates the whole torso and skims over the midsection on its way down. A fine-gauge cardigan left open, worn over a V-neck layer, does something similar by creating a long vertical line down the front. Thick, chunky, or cropped knits are worth being selective with — heavier yarn adds bulk exactly where you’re usually trying to minimise it, and a cropped length stops right at the widest point instead of moving past it.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-knitwear-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of knitwear on an apple body shape — avoid: chunky turtleneck knit (heavy texture right at the fullest point); choose: longline fine-knit v-neck (drapes past the middle in one clean line)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="apple-jackets">Jackets</h3>
      <p>Jacket length is doing most of the work in this category. A <strong>longline blazer or duster coat</strong>, left open rather than buttoned, creates a continuous vertical line from shoulder to thigh that carries the eye straight past the midsection. An open, unstructured cardigan-style jacket works the same way for something more casual. Cropped jackets and boleros are the length to watch — anything that ends right at or just above the natural waist draws a horizontal line exactly at the point you’re usually trying to soften, which has the opposite effect of a longer style worn open.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-jackets-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of jackets on an apple body shape — avoid: cropped bolero jacket (ends right at the widest part of the torso); choose: longline open blazer (two vertical lines that extend past the middle)">
      </figure>
      <h3 id="apple-coats">Coats</h3>
      <p>Leave the belt off your outerwear. A wrap coat tied at the waist cinches the fullest part of the torso, which is the reverse of what this shape needs. A <strong>straight, single-breasted coat</strong> worn open — or a longline duster — falls in a clean column from the shoulder and draws two long vertical lines down the front of the body. Aim for a hem at the knee or below and a fabric with some weight (wool, heavy crepe, a lined trench cotton) so it hangs from the shoulders instead of following the midsection.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-coats-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of coats on an apple body shape — avoid: belted wrap coat (the belt cinches the widest part of the torso); choose: straight single-breasted coat (falls in a clean column from the shoulder)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="apple-trousers">Trousers</h3>
      <p>A <strong>high-rise waistband</strong> is one of the most useful single choices available to this shape, since it holds the midsection in gently and extends the visual line of the torso upward before the leg even begins. Straight-leg or slightly wide-leg trousers in a structured fabric keep the rest of the silhouette clean and balanced underneath. Low-rise cuts are the ones to reconsider — a waistband that sits below the natural midsection point means fabric and seams collect right at the fullest part of the torso, which draws attention there rather than smoothing past it.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-trousers-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of trousers on an apple body shape — avoid: low-rise trousers (sits under the fullest part of the midsection); choose: high-rise straight leg (holds and smooths, then falls in a long line)">
      </figure>
      <h3 id="apple-jeans">Jeans</h3>
      <p>Apple shapes often have slimmer legs than the upper body suggests, and jeans are the easiest place to use that. A <strong>mid-rise bootcut</strong> or straight leg sits flat below the midsection and adds a little width at the hem to balance the top half. Look for a contoured or wider waistband and a little stretch, so the band lies flat rather than digging in. Low-rise skinny jeans combine both problems at once: the waistband cuts in under the fullest point and the leg narrows away beneath it, which makes the torso read heavier than it is.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-jeans-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of jeans on an apple body shape — avoid: low-rise skinny jeans (waistband digs in, leg narrows under a fuller middle); choose: mid-rise bootcut jeans (balances the upper body with a little width at the hem)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="apple-skirts">Skirts</h3>
      <p>An <strong>A-line skirt</strong>, sitting at or just above the natural waist, is close to a universal recommendation for this shape because it skims outward from the body instead of clinging to it at any point. Paired with a top that has some vertical detail — a placket, a seam, an open cardigan — it builds one long line from shoulder to hem. A tight pencil skirt is the more difficult choice here, purely because the fitted fabric has nowhere to go but follow the body’s exact shape through the midsection, with none of the ease an A-line naturally builds in.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-skirts-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of skirts on an apple body shape — avoid: tight pencil skirt (clings to the midsection with no ease); choose: a-line skirt (skims from waist to hem without clinging)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="apple-dresses">Dresses</h3>
      <p>An <strong>empire-waist dress</strong> is one of the most reliably flattering silhouettes for this shape, because the seam sits just under the bust — above the midsection entirely — and everything below it falls loosely to the hem. That single design choice does most of the work: there’s a defined point on the body, and it isn’t the one you’re usually trying to minimise. A wrap dress in a fabric with good drape is a close second. Bodycon and other clingy, fitted-throughout dresses are the hardest category here, simply because there’s no point in the garment that isn’t following the body closely.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-dresses-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of dresses on an apple body shape — avoid: bodycon dress (fitted everywhere, including the midsection); choose: empire-waist dress (marks the narrow line under the bust, skims past the middle)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="apple-shorts">Shorts</h3>
      <p>Structured fabric does more here than cut alone — a pair of <strong>tailored shorts</strong> in a fabric with a little body (cotton twill, a light linen blend) holds its own shape rather than collapsing onto the midsection the way soft, clingy fabric does. A mid-rise waistband, neither cutting across the fullest point nor sitting so high it emphasises it, is usually the most comfortable balance. Tight bike shorts or anything in a very stretchy, thin jersey are the ones to be selective with, since that fabric follows the body’s exact shape from waistband to hem with essentially no ease built in.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-shorts-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of shorts on an apple body shape — avoid: tight bike shorts (clings from waist to thigh with no ease); choose: tailored mid-rise shorts (straight leg, sits flat at the front)">
      </figure>
      <h3 id="apple-jumpsuits-and-playsuits">Jumpsuits &amp; Playsuits</h3>
      <p>Define the empire line, not the waist. A jumpsuit with a <strong>V-neck and a seam just under the bust</strong>, falling loose to a wide leg, gives you a defined point at your narrowest spot and room through the middle. Soft, drapey fabric matters more here than in any other category — crepe and viscose move past the midsection, stiff cotton stands away from it. A tight jumpsuit with a belt at the natural waist is the combination to avoid: it outlines the midsection and then draws a line across it.</p>
      <figure class="abs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/apple-jumpsuits-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of jumpsuits on an apple body shape — avoid: tight, belted jumpsuit (fitted and cinched at the fullest point); choose: empire v-neck wide-leg (defined under the bust, loose through the middle)">
      </figure>

    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-measure">
      <h2>Common Apple Shape Measurements by Size</h2>
      <p>The pattern holds at every size — bust and hips close together, waist close behind or level with them:</p>
